            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is already quite well sealed, and unless you are noticing drafts, I would not recommend any major air sealing work at this time. However, as we did discuss that you are planning to replace some or all of the insulation in your basement header, I do recommend that you do air seal prior to replacing the insulation. 



Several of my other upgrade recommendations, such as an upgrade to your heating system, will also affect your air sealing. 



I also recommend you replace your bathroom fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $45&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1 inch of exterior rigid foam board insualation on the exterior, for an approximate total insulation value of R-15. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, for a total insulation value of R-31.



The wall between your house and the unconditioned back porch is assumed to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. While your porch provides a buffer between the outdoors and the indoors, I recommend adding 3 inches of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to this wall as well.  



As discussed during my site visit, this upgrade is often not cost-effective unless it is done at the same time you have to replace your siding. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the exterior walls and the wall between the porch and your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $470&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;15.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 8 inches (R24) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding an additional R32 of insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R32 to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $275&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$375 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete 2x4 framing and R12 insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation on top of the 2x4 framing.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $710&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;23.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of your windows are already triple pane windows, but do not appear to have a Low E coating, or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, However, since they are all hinged windows in good physical condition, I do not recommend replacing them at this time.



I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading your large front window and old basement windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing these windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $160&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

HRV



As previously mentioned, your home is already quite airtight, and may in fact be underventilated. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



This upgrade is particularly important to consider if you are noticing air quality issues in your home, or if you are planning any upgrades that may affect the air tightness of your home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;spend $30&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182L electric hot water tank. I have modelled two different options for you to consider.



The first option is to add a drain water heat recovery (DWHR) unit to your existing hot water tank. This unit replaces a section of drain pipe from your showers and uses the heat from the hot water going down the drain to pre-heat the water going into your electric hot water tank. 



I have recommended this upgrade because your hot water tank is located directly below your two bathrooms, and the piping is relatively accessible. Different configurations are possible, so if interested please speak to a contractor. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a drain water heat recovery unit in your home, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $340&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





Your second option, should you decide to switch to a propane fired furnace, you could replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

As with your domestic hot water, I have modeleld two separate options for upgrading your heating system. 



Your first option is to upgrade your oil furnace to a more efficient oil furnace. 



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $315&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. Because the efficiency is only 87%, there would be no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;





Your second option is to replace your oil fired furnace with a propane fired furnace. 



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $970&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;29.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace 1 toilets. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



